= Nobel Prize in Physics
{c}
{wiki}

Official award records: https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/lists/all-nobel-prizes-in-physics/

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2026
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Francis Halzen> received the full award for decisive contributions to <IceCube> and the discovery of high-energy <neutrinos> from astrophysical sources.https://www.kva.se/en/news/the-nobel-prize-in-physics-2026/[6 October 2026 announcement, award decision and motivation, lines 60–65]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2025
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<John Clarke>, <Michel H. Devoret> and <John M. Martinis> shared the award equally for discovering macroscopic <quantum tunnelling> and quantized energy levels in an <electric circuit>.https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2025/summary/[Individual laureate entries, one-third shares and joint motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2024
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<John J. Hopfield> and <Geoffrey Hinton> shared the award equally for foundational discoveries and inventions enabling <machine learning> with <artificial neural networks>.https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2024/summary/[Individual laureate entries, half shares and joint motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2023
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Pierre Agostini>, <Ferenc Krausz> and <Anne L’Huillier> shared the award equally for experimental methods generating <attosecond> light pulses to study <electron> dynamics in matter.https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2023/summary/[Individual laureate entries, one-third shares and joint motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2022
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Alain Aspect>, <John F. Clauser> and <Anton Zeilinger> shared the award equally for experiments with entangled <photons>, violations of <Bell inequalities> and pioneering <quantum information science>.https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2022/summary/[Individual laureate entries, one-third shares and joint motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2021
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Syukuro Manabe> and <Klaus Hasselmann> each received one quarter for physical <climate models> quantifying variability and predicting <global warming>. <Giorgio Parisi> received one half for discovering how disorder and fluctuations interact in physical systems from atomic to planetary scales.https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2021/summary/[Individual laureate entries, quarter/quarter/half shares and two distinct award contributions]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2020
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Roger Penrose> for showing that <black hole> formation is a robust prediction of <general relativity>. One quarter each went to <Reinhard Genzel> and <Andrea Ghez> for discovering a supermassive compact object at the centre of the <Milky Way>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2020 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Penrose one half for robust relativistic black-hole formation; Genzel and Ghez one quarter each for the Galactic-centre compact object.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2019
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <James Peebles> for theoretical discoveries in physical <cosmology>. One quarter each went to <Michel Mayor> and <Didier Queloz> for discovering an <exoplanet> orbiting a solar-type <star>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2019 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Peebles one half for physical cosmology; Mayor and Queloz one quarter each for an exoplanet around a solar-type star.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2018
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Arthur Ashkin> for <optical tweezers> and their biological applications. One quarter each went to <Gérard Mourou> and <Donna Strickland> for a method of generating intense, ultrashort optical pulses.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2018 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Ashkin one half for optical tweezers and biological applications; Mourou and Strickland one quarter each for intense ultrashort optical pulses.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2017
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Rainer Weiss>, and one quarter each to <Barry C. Barish> and <Kip S. Thorne>, for decisive contributions to the <LIGO> detector and observation of <gravitational waves>.<Press release: The Nobel Prize in Physics 2017 by Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences>[Official 3 October 2017 award decision: Weiss one half, remaining half jointly to Barish and Thorne; their individual official facts records explicitly give one quarter each.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2016
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <David J. Thouless>, and one quarter each to <F. Duncan M. Haldane> and <J. Michael Kosterlitz>, for theoretical discoveries concerning topological <phase transitions> and <topological quantum matter>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2016 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Thouless one half; Haldane and Kosterlitz one quarter each for topological phase transitions and phases of matter.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2015
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Takaaki Kajita> and <Arthur B. McDonald> for discovering <neutrino oscillations>, showing that <neutrinos> have <mass>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2015 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Kajita and McDonald one half each; neutrino oscillations establish neutrino mass.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2014
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal thirds to <Isamu Akasaki>, <Hiroshi Amano> and <Shuji Nakamura> for inventing efficient blue <light-emitting diodes> enabling bright, energy-saving white light sources.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2014 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura one third each; efficient blue LEDs enabling white lighting.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2013
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <François Englert> and <Peter W. Higgs> for the theoretical discovery of a mechanism explaining the origin of <mass> in <subatomic particles>, confirmed by discovery of the predicted particle in the <ATLAS experiment> and <CMS experiment> at the <Large Hadron Collider>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2013 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Englert and Higgs one half each; theoretical mass mechanism and its experimental confirmation.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2012
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Serge Haroche> and <David J. Wineland> for experimental methods enabling measurement and manipulation of individual <quantum systems>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2012 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Haroche and Wineland one half each; measurement and manipulation of individual quantum systems.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2011
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Saul Perlmutter>, and one quarter each to <Brian P. Schmidt> and <Adam G. Riess>, for discovering the <accelerating expansion of the universe> through observations of distant <supernovae>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2011 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Perlmutter one half; Schmidt and Riess one quarter each; cosmic acceleration from distant supernovae.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2010
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Andre Geim> and <Konstantin Novoselov> for pioneering experiments on the two-dimensional material <graphene>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2010 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Geim and Novoselov one half each; experiments on two-dimensional graphene.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2009
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Charles Kuen Kao> for breakthroughs in transmitting light through <optical fibers> for communication. One quarter each went to <Willard S. Boyle> and <George E. Smith> for inventing the <charge-coupled device> imaging sensor.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2009 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 2009 summary: Kao one half for optical-fiber communication; Boyle and Smith one quarter each for the CCD imaging sensor.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2008
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Yoichiro Nambu> for discovering the mechanism of <spontaneous symmetry breaking> in subatomic <physics>. One quarter each went to <Makoto Kobayashi> and <Toshihide Maskawa> for explaining broken symmetry through a mechanism requiring at least three families of <quarks>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2008 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 2008 summary: Nambu one half; Kobayashi and Maskawa one quarter each, with the two distinct symmetry-breaking contributions specified.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2007
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Albert Fert> and <Peter Grünberg> for discovering <giant magnetoresistance>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2007 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 2007 summary: Fert and Grünberg one half each for giant magnetoresistance.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2006
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <John C. Mather> and <George F. Smoot> for discovering the <black-body radiation> form and <anisotropy> of the <cosmic microwave background>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2006 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 2006 summary: Mather and Smoot one half each; blackbody form and anisotropy of cosmic microwave background radiation.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2005
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Roy J. Glauber> for contributions to the <quantum theory> of <optical coherence>. One quarter each went to <John L. Hall> and <Theodor W. Hänsch> for developing precision <laser spectroscopy>, including the <optical frequency comb> technique.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2005 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 2005 summary: Glauber one half for quantum optical coherence; Hall and Hänsch one quarter each for laser-based precision spectroscopy including frequency combs.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2004
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal thirds to <David J. Gross>, <H. David Politzer> and <Frank Wilczek> for discovering <asymptotic freedom> in the theory of the <strong interaction>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2004 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 2004 summary: Gross, Politzer and Wilczek one third each for asymptotic freedom in the strong interaction.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2003
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal thirds to <Alexei Alexeyevich Abrikosov>, <Vitaly Lazarevich Ginzburg> and <Anthony J. Leggett> for pioneering theoretical contributions to <superconductors> and <superfluids>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2003 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 2003 summary: equal thirds; theory of superconductors and superfluids.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2002
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One quarter each went to <Raymond Davis Jr.> and <Masatoshi Koshiba> for pioneering <astrophysics> through detection of cosmic <neutrinos>. One half went to <Riccardo Giacconi> for pioneering <astrophysics> leading to the discovery of <cosmic X-ray sources>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2002 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 2002 summary: Davis and Koshiba one quarter each for cosmic neutrinos; Giacconi one half for cosmic X-ray sources.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2001
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal thirds to <Eric A. Cornell>, <Wolfgang Ketterle> and <Carl E. Wieman> for achieving <Bose-Einstein condensation> in dilute <alkali atom> gases and investigating the resulting condensates.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2001 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 2001 summary: equal thirds; dilute alkali-gas condensation and early fundamental studies of condensate properties.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 2000
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

For foundational work in information and communication technology, <Zhores I. Alferov> and <Herbert Kroemer> received one quarter each for developing <semiconductor heterostructures> used in high-speed electronics and <optoelectronics>. <Jack S. Kilby> received one half for his part in inventing the <integrated circuit>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 2000 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 2000 summary: Alferov and Kroemer one quarter each for semiconductor heterostructures; Kilby one half for integrated circuits.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1999
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Gerardus 't Hooft> and <Martinus J. G. Veltman> for explaining the <quantum field theory> of <electroweak interactions>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1999 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1999 summary: equal halves; quantum structure of electroweak interactions.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1998
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal thirds to <Robert B. Laughlin>, <Horst L. Störmer> and <Daniel C. Tsui> for discovering a new <quantum fluid> with fractionally charged excitations.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1998 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1998 summary: equal thirds; new quantum fluid with fractionally charged excitations.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1997
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal thirds to <Steven Chu>, <Claude Cohen-Tannoudji> and <William D. Phillips> for developing methods to cool and trap <chemical atoms> using <lasers>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1997 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1997 summary: three one-third shares; methods for cooling and trapping atoms with laser light.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1996
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal thirds to <David M. Lee>, <Douglas D. Osheroff> and <Robert C. Richardson> for discovering <superfluidity> in <helium-3>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1996 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1996 summary: three one-third shares; discovery of superfluidity in helium-3.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1995
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

For pioneering experimental contributions to lepton physics, one half went to <Martin Lewis Perl> for discovering the <tau lepton>; the other half went to <Frederick Reines> for detecting the <neutrino>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1995 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1995 summary: equal halves; tau-lepton discovery versus neutrino detection.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1994
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

For pioneering <neutron scattering> techniques for studying <condensed matter>, <Bertram Neville Brockhouse> received one half for developing <neutron spectroscopy>, while <Clifford Glenwood Shull> received one half for developing <neutron diffraction>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1994 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1994 summary: equal halves; Brockhouse neutron spectroscopy and Shull neutron diffraction are distinct contributions.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1993
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Russell Alan Hulse> and <Joseph Hooton Taylor Jr.> for discovering a new type of <pulsar> that opened new possibilities for studying <gravity>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1993 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1993 summary: equal halves; discovery of a new pulsar type enabling gravitation studies.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1992
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Georges Charpak> for inventing and developing <particle detectors>, especially the <multiwire proportional chamber>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1992 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official 14 October 1992 award decision names Georges Charpak alone; invention and development of particle detectors, particularly the multiwire proportional chamber.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1991
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Pierre-Gilles de Gennes> for extending methods for studying ordering in simple systems to complex matter, especially <liquid crystals> and <polymers>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1991 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1991 summary: sole laureate; ordering methods extended to liquid crystals and polymers.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1990
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal thirds to <Jerome Isaac Friedman>, <Henry Way Kendall> and <Richard Edward Taylor> for investigations of <deep inelastic scattering> of <electrons> from <protons> and bound <neutrons>, essential to developing the <quark model>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1990 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1990 summary: three one-third shares; deep inelastic electron scattering from protons and bound neutrons and its importance to the quark model.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1989
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Norman Foster Ramsey, Jr.> received one half for inventing the separated oscillatory fields method and applying it to the <hydrogen maser> and other <atomic clocks>. <Hans G. Dehmelt> and <Wolfgang Paul> received one quarter each for developing <ion trap> techniques.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1989 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1989 summary: Ramsey one half, Dehmelt and Paul one quarter each; separated fields and clocks versus ion trapping.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1988
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal thirds to <Leon Max Lederman>, <Melvin Schwartz> and <Jack Steinberger> for the neutrino-beam method and demonstrating the doublet structure of <leptons> through discovery of the <muon neutrino>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1988 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official Steinberger facts: one-third share, three named laureates and neutrino-beam/lepton-doublet motivation; the equal three-person partition follows from the official Nobel Foundation rule.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1987
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Johannes Georg Bednorz> and <Karl Alexander Müller> for the breakthrough discovery of <superconductivity> in ceramic materials.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1987 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1987 summary: equal halves; breakthrough discovery of superconductivity in ceramic materials.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1986
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Ernst Ruska> received one half for fundamental work in <electron optics> and designing the first <electron microscope>. <Gerd Binnig> and <Heinrich Rohrer> received one quarter each for designing the <scanning tunneling microscope>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1986 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1986 summary: Ruska one half for electron optics and the first electron microscope; Binnig and Rohrer one quarter each for scanning tunneling microscopy.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1985
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Klaus von Klitzing> for discovering the <quantum Hall effect>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1985 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1985 summary: sole laureate, quantized Hall-effect discovery.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1984
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Carlo Rubbia> and <Simon van der Meer> for decisive contributions to the project that discovered the $W$ and $Z$ particles mediating the <weak interaction>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1984 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1984 summary: equal halves; decisive contributions to the project discovering W and Z, communicators of weak interaction.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1983
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar> for theoretical studies of processes governing stellar structure and evolution. The other half went to <William Alfred Fowler> for theoretical and experimental studies of <nuclear reactions> involved in forming the <chemical elements> in the universe.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1983 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1983 summary: equal halves; stellar structure/evolution and nuclear reactions forming chemical elements are distinct motivations.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1982
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Kenneth Geddes Wilson> for his theory of <critical phenomena> associated with <phase transitions>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1982 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1982 summary: sole laureate, theory of critical phenomena and phase transitions; official summary uses Kenneth G. Wilson, expanded name verified separately in worker professional metadata.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1981
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Nicolaas Bloembergen> and <Arthur Leonard Schawlow> received one quarter each for developing <laser spectroscopy>. <Kai Manne Börje Siegbahn> received one half for developing high-resolution <electron spectroscopy>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1981 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual 1981 record reproduced in laser-physics lecture slides: Bloembergen and Schawlow one-quarter each, laser spectroscopy; Siegbahn one-half, electron spectroscopy. Official individual Siegbahn facts independently confirms his half and distinct motivation.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1980
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <James Watson Cronin> and <Val Logsdon Fitch> for discovering violations of fundamental <symmetry> principles in neutral <kaon> decay.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1980 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official 1980 summary: equal halves; fundamental symmetry violations in neutral K-meson decay.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1979
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Sheldon Lee Glashow>, <Abdus Salam> and <Steven Weinberg> for contributions to unified <weak interactions> and <electromagnetic interactions>, including the prediction of the <neutral current>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1979 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: three equal thirds; unified weak and electromagnetic interactions and predicted neutral current.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1978
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Pyotr Leonidovich Kapitza> for inventions and discoveries in low-temperature <physics>. <Arno Allan Penzias> and <Robert Woodrow Wilson> received one quarter each for discovering the <cosmic microwave background>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1978 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Kapitza one-half for low-temperature work; Penzias and Wilson one-quarter each for cosmic microwave background discovery.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1977
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Philip Warren Anderson>, <Nevill Francis Mott> and <John Hasbrouck Van Vleck> for theoretical investigations of electronic structure in magnetic and disordered systems.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1977 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Three individual official laureate records: each one-third, same electronic-structure/magnetic/disordered-system motivation.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1976
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Burton Richter> and <Samuel Chao Chung Ting> for pioneering work discovering a new kind of heavy <elementary particle>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1976 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: equal halves for pioneering discovery of a heavy elementary particle of a new kind.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1975
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Aage Niels Bohr>, <Ben Roy Mottelson> and <Leo James Rainwater> for connecting collective motion with individual-particle motion in <atomic nuclei> and developing a theory of <nuclear structure> from that connection.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1975 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Reproduced individual official 1975 record: CERN-hosted slide 8 gives all three full names and one-third shares; IN2P3-hosted slide reproduces full individual official motivation and its canonical URL.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1974
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Martin Ryle> and <Antony Hewish> for pioneering radio <astrophysics>: Ryle for observations and inventions, especially <aperture synthesis>, and Hewish for his decisive role in discovering <pulsars>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1974 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: equal halves; Ryle aperture synthesis and Hewish decisive pulsar-discovery role distinguished.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1973
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half was shared by <Leo Esaki> and <Ivar Giaever> for experimental discoveries of <quantum tunnelling> in <semiconductors> and <superconductivity>, respectively. The other half went to <Brian D. Josephson> for predicting supercurrents across a tunnel barrier and the <Josephson effect>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1973 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: Esaki and Giaever one-quarter each, Josephson one-half; experimental and theoretical motivations separately.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1972
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <John Bardeen>, <Leon Neil Cooper> and <John Robert Schrieffer> for their jointly developed theory of <superconductivity>, the <BCS theory>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1972 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: all three laureates, equal one-third shares, joint superconductivity theory.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1971
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Dennis Gabor> for inventing and developing <holography>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1971 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: sole laureate and holographic-method invention/development motivation.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1970
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Hannes Olof Gösta Alfvén> for work in <magnetohydrodynamics> and its applications in <plasma> physics, and <Louis Eugène Félix Néel> for discoveries concerning <antiferromagnetism> and <ferrimagnetism> and their applications in solid-state physics.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1970 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official summary: equal halves; magnetohydrodynamics/plasma and antiferromagnetism/ferrimagnetism motivations distinct.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1969
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Murray Gell-Mann> for discoveries concerning the classification of <elementary particles> and their interactions.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1969 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Reproduced individual official 1969 record: sole laureate, classification of elementary particles and their interactions.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1968
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Luis Walter Alvarez> for discoveries in <elementary particle> physics, especially particle resonances identified using <hydrogen> <bubble chambers> and analysis of their records.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1968 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Reproduced individual official 1968 record: sole laureate, resonance discoveries using hydrogen bubble chambers and data analysis.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1967
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Hans Albrecht Bethe> for contributions to the theory of <nuclear reactions>, especially explaining energy production in <stars>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1967 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Slide 13: reproduced individual official 1967 Nobel record; sole laureate and nuclear-reaction/stellar-energy motivation.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1966
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Alfred Kastler> for discovering and developing optical methods for studying radiofrequency resonances in <chemical atoms>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1966 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official award summary: sole laureate and optical methods for Hertzian resonances in atoms.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1965
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Sin-Itiro Tomonaga>, <Julian Seymour Schwinger> and <Richard Feynman> for fundamental work in <quantum electrodynamics>, with consequences for <elementary particles>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1965 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official award summary: all three laureates, one-third shares, and common quantum-electrodynamics motivation.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1964
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Charles Hard Townes>; the other half jointly to <Nicolay Gennadiyevich Basov> and <Aleksandr Mikhailovich Prokhorov>, for quantum-electronics work leading to oscillators and amplifiers using the <maser>-<laser> principle.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1964 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official award summary: Townes one-half; Basov and Prokhorov one-quarter each; common quantum-electronics motivation.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1963
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Eugene Paul Wigner> for theories of <atomic nuclei> and <elementary particles>, particularly <symmetry> principles; the other half jointly to <Maria Goeppert Mayer> and <Johannes Hans Daniel Jensen> for discoveries about the <nuclear shell model>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1963 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Reproduced individual official Nobel record: Wigner one-half for nuclear/particle theory and symmetry; Mayer and Jensen one-quarter each for nuclear shell structure.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1962
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Lev Davidovich Landau> for pioneering theories of condensed matter, especially liquid <helium>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1962 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official award summary: sole laureate and condensed-matter/liquid-helium theory motivation.]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1961
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Robert Hofstadter> for <electron scattering> studies of <atomic nuclei> and discoveries about <nucleon> structure, and <Rudolf Ludwig Mössbauer> for resonance absorption of <gamma rays> and the <Mössbauer effect>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1961 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Both laureates, equal shares and separate prize motivations]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1960
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Donald Arthur Glaser> for inventing the <bubble chamber>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1960 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Award year, laureate and bubble-chamber invention motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1959
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded jointly to <Emilio Segrè> and <Owen Chamberlain> for discovering the <antiproton>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1959 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Both laureates, equal shares and antiproton-discovery motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1958
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded jointly to <Pavel Alekseyevich Cherenkov>, <Ilya Mikhailovich Frank> and <Igor Yevgenyevich Tamm> for discovering and interpreting <Cherenkov radiation>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1958 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official award motivation and presentation, printed pp. 419, 421–425; presentation closing names all three recipients]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1957
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded jointly to <Chen Ning Yang> and <Tsung-Dao Lee> for investigating <parity conservation> laws, leading to discoveries about <elementary particles>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1957 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Both laureates, equal shares and parity-law investigation motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1956
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded jointly to <William Bradford Shockley>, <John Bardeen> and <Walter Houser Brattain> for research on <semiconductors> and discovery of the <transistor> effect.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1956 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[All three laureates, equal shares and semiconductor/transistor motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1955
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Willis Eugene Lamb Jr.> for discoveries concerning the <fine structure> of the <hydrogen> spectrum and <Polykarp Kusch> for precision determination of the <electron> <magnetic moment>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1955 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official motivation, printed p. 281; PDF page 273, followed by presentation pp. 283–285]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1954
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Max Born> for fundamental <quantum mechanics> research, especially the statistical interpretation of the <wave function>, and <Walther Bothe> for the <coincidence circuit> and discoveries made with it.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1954 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Award year, both laureates, equal shares and separate prize motivations]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1953
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Frits Zernike> for the <phase-contrast microscopy> method, particularly the <phase-contrast microscope>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1953 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Award year, laureate and prize motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1952
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded jointly to <Felix Bloch> and <Edward Mills Purcell> for new methods of precise nuclear magnetic measurement and related discoveries. The <nuclear magnetic resonance> article explains their distinct experiments.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1952 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official motivation, printed p. 197; PDF page 195, followed by presentation pp. 199–202]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1951
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded jointly to <John Douglas Cockcroft> and <Ernest Thomas Sinton Walton> for pioneering <nuclear reactions> of <atomic nuclei> using artificially accelerated <particles>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1951 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Individual official motivation, printed p. 161; PDF page 160, followed by presentation pp. 163–166]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1950
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Cecil Frank Powell> for developing the photographic method for studying <nuclear reactions> and discoveries about <mesons> made with it.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1950 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Award year, laureate and prize motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1949
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Hideki Yukawa> for predicting <mesons> through theoretical work on <nuclear forces>.<Hideki Yukawa - Facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Award year, laureate and prize motivation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1948
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Patrick Maynard Stuart Blackett> for developing the <cloud chamber> method and discoveries in <nuclear physics> and <cosmic ray> research. The <Blackett-Occhialini triggered cloud-chamber experiment> follows one of those methods.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1948 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureate, year and stated scientific subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1947
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Edward Victor Appleton> for upper-atmosphere investigations, especially the Appleton layer. The <ionosphere> connects this work to radio propagation.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1947 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureate, year and stated scientific subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1946
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Percy Williams Bridgman> for high-pressure apparatus and discoveries made with it. The <pressure> article develops the associated measurement problems.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1946 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureate, year and stated scientific subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1945
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Wolfgang Pauli> for the <Pauli exclusion principle>, connecting atomic spectroscopy with allowed arrangements of <fermions>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1945 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureate, year and stated scientific subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1944
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Isidor Isaac Rabi> for the resonance method of recording magnetic properties of <atomic nuclei>. The <Rabi cycle> article explains a driven-state model and the beam experiment.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1944 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureate, year and stated scientific subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1943
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Otto Stern> for the <molecular beam> method and discovery of the <proton> <magnetic moment>. The <molecular beam> article develops direct measurements of particle speeds.<Otto Stern - Facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureate, year and stated scientific subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1942
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

No physics prize was awarded this year.<Nobel Prize facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Years without Nobel Prizes: Physics, 1942]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1941
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

No physics prize was awarded this year.<Nobel Prize facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Years without Nobel Prizes: Physics, 1941]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1940
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

No physics prize was awarded this year.<Nobel Prize facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Years without Nobel Prizes: Physics, 1940]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1939
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Ernest Orlando Lawrence> for the <cyclotron> and results obtained with it, connecting repeated acceleration to nuclear experiments.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1939 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureate, year and stated scientific subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1938
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Enrico Fermi> for neutron-induced radioactivity and reactions brought about by slow <neutrons>. The <neutron> article follows the activation experiments and their interpretation.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1938 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureate, year and stated scientific subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1937
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Clinton Davisson> and <George Paget Thomson> shared the award equally for demonstrating <electron diffraction> by <crystals>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1937 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official summary: laureates, prize shares and motivation]{ref} The <Davisson–Germer experiment> and <Thomson electron transmission diffraction experiment> connect different apparatus geometries to the <matter waves>.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1936
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Victor Franz Hess> for <cosmic rays> and <Carl David Anderson> for the <positron>. The <Hess cosmic-ray balloon experiment> and <Anderson positron track experiment> explain the measurements.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1936 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Hess and Anderson, half shares, cosmic radiation and positron]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1935
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <James Chadwick> for discovering the <neutron>. The <Chadwick neutron discovery experiment> follows the recoil measurements and competing interpretations.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1935 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Chadwick and neutron discovery]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1934
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

No physics prize was awarded this year.<Nobel Prize facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Years without Nobel Prizes: Physics, 1934]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1933
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded jointly to <Erwin Schrödinger> and <Paul Dirac> for developments in atomic theory.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1933 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Joint award: Erwin Schrödinger and Paul Adrien Maurice Dirac, each receiving one half]{ref}

The <Schrödinger equation> and relativistic theory of the <electron> are two routes through that work. The <Davisson–Germer experiment> supplies related experimental evidence for <matter waves>.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1932
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Werner Heisenberg> for creating <quantum mechanics>, including its application to forms of <hydrogen>. The <Heisenberg uncertainty relation> is one route into the resulting theory.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1932 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureate, year and stated scientific subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1931
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

No physics prize was awarded this year.<Nobel Prize facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Years without Nobel Prizes: Physics, 1931]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1930
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman> for light scattering and the effect bearing his name. The <Raman and Krishnan light-scattering experiment> includes the work of <Kariamanikkam Srinivasa Krishnan>, who shared the original paper.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1930 by Nobel Foundation>[Official award record: Raman and light scattering; collaborator attribution from the separately cited original experiment]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1929
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Louis de Broglie> for the wave nature of <electrons>. The <de Broglie wavelength> connects <momentum> to observable <diffraction>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1929 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: de Broglie and electron matter waves]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1928
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Owen Willans Richardson> for <thermionic emission> and its emission law. He received the prize in 1929.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1928 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: thermionic emission, Richardson and deferred receipt in 1929]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1927
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded in equal shares to <Arthur Holly Compton> for the <Compton effect> and <Charles Thomson Rees Wilson> for making charged-particle paths visible by <condensation>. The <Compton scattering experiment of 1923> and <Wilson cloud-chamber experiment> explain the two experimental methods.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1927 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Compton and Wilson, half shares and their distinct experimental subjects]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1926
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Jean Baptiste Perrin> for the discontinuous structure of matter, especially <sedimentation equilibrium>. The <Perrin sedimentation experiment> describes the measurements.<Jean Baptiste Perrin - Facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award facts: Perrin, 1926 and sedimentation equilibrium]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1925
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded jointly to <James Franck> and <Gustav Ludwig Hertz> for electron-impact laws in <chemical atoms>. They received the prize in 1926; the <Franck-Hertz experiment> explains the evidence.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1925 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Franck and Hertz, electron impact and deferred receipt in 1926]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1924
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Karl Manne Georg Siegbahn> for <X-ray spectroscopy>. He received the prize in 1925. The <Siegbahn precision X-ray wavelength spectroscopy> article describes its calibration problems.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1924 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Manne Siegbahn, X-ray spectroscopy and deferred receipt in 1925]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1923
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Robert Andrews Millikan> for the <elementary charge> and <photoelectric effect>. The <oil drop experiment> and <Millikan photoelectric experiment> follow the two measurements.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1923 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Millikan, elementary charge and photoelectric effect]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1922
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Niels Bohr> for atomic structure and radiation from <chemical atoms>. The <Bohr model> explains the connection to discrete <spectral lines>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1922 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Bohr, atomic structure and radiation]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1921
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Albert Einstein> for theoretical physics, with particular recognition of the law of the <photoelectric effect>. He received the award in 1922.<Albert Einstein, Facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Prize motivation and delayed presentation]{ref}

The <Millikan photoelectric experiment> makes the stopping-voltage test and its experimental limitations explicit.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1920
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Charles-Édouard Guillaume> for precision measurement through anomalous properties of nickel–steel <alloys>. The <Guillaume thermal expansion experiment> explains how a reference comparison reveals low <thermal expansion>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1920 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Guillaume and precision measurement with nickel–steel alloys]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1919
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Johannes Stark> for the <Doppler effect> in <canal rays> and the splitting of <spectral lines> in <electric fields>. The <Stark canal-ray experiment> follows the measurement.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1919 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Stark, canal-ray Doppler effect and electric-field splitting]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1918
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Max Planck> for the discovery of energy quanta; he received it in 1919. <Planck's law> connects quantization with the measured spectrum of <black-body radiation>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1918 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Planck, energy quanta and deferred receipt in 1919]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1917
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Charles Glover Barkla> for characteristic <X-rays> of the <chemical elements>; he received it in 1918. The <Barkla characteristic X-ray experiment> describes the experimental separation of components.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1917 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: Barkla, characteristic radiation and deferred receipt in 1918]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1916
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

No award was made that year.<Facts on the Nobel Prize in Physics by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Years without an award: 1916 is one of the six listed]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1915
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded jointly to <William Henry Bragg> and <William Lawrence Bragg> for methods of determining <crystal structures> using <X-rays>. <Bragg's law> connects measured angles to lattice-plane spacings.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1915 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureates and crystal-structure subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1914
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Max von Laue> for the discovery of <X-ray diffraction> by crystals.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1914 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: laureate and diffraction subject]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1913
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Heike Kamerlingh Onnes> for investigations of matter at low <temperatures>, including <helium liquefaction>. The award's scope includes the experimental means of reaching those conditions.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1913 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: low-temperature properties and liquid-helium production]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1912
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

Awarded to <Gustaf Dalén> for automatic regulators used with gas accumulators for lighthouse and buoy lighting. The <sun valve> and <Dalén light> connect the award to mechanical engineering.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1912 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official award record: automatic regulators and marine lighting]{ref}

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1911
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Wilhelm Wien> received the award for discoveries concerning the laws of <thermal radiation>.<All Nobel Prizes in Physics by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Physics 1911: laureate and award motivation]{ref} <Wien's displacement law> connects a <blackbody spectrum>'s peak <wavelength> to its <temperature>.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1910
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Johannes Diderik van der Waals> received the award for an <equation of state> describing gases and liquids.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1910 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official summary: laureates, prize shares and motivation]{ref} The <Van der Waals equation> connects that description to a <Van der Waals critical point> and <Van der Waals liquid-gas coexistence>.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1909
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Guglielmo Marconi> and <Karl Ferdinand Braun> shared the award equally for developing <wireless telegraphy>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1909 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official summary: laureates, prize shares and motivation]{ref} <Braun's receiver coupling experiment> connects that history to <resonance> and the selectivity of a receiver.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1908
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Gabriel Lippmann> received the award for a photographic colour-recording method based on <interference>.<All Nobel Prizes in Physics by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Physics 1908: laureate and award motivation]{ref} The <Lippmann colour-recording experiment> stores a <standing wave> pattern in a photographic layer.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1907
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Albert Abraham Michelson> received the award for precise optical instruments and their use in <spectroscopy> and <metrology>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1907 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official summary: laureates, prize shares and motivation]{ref} The <Michelson interferometer> converts relative <wave phase> into a readout for precise <length> measurements.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1906
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Joseph John Thomson> received the award for theoretical and experimental research on <electric currents> through <gases>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1906 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official summary: laureates, prize shares and motivation]{ref} The <Thomson charge-to-mass experiment> connects <cathode rays> to the measurable <charge-to-mass ratio> of an <electron>.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1905
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Philipp Lenard> received the award for research on <cathode rays>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1905 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official summary: laureates, prize shares and motivation]{ref} The <Lenard cathode-ray window experiment> separates the source region from the region where the emerging <electrons> are studied.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1904
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Lord Rayleigh> received the award for gas-<mass density> investigations and the discovery of <argon>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1904 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official summary: laureates, prize shares and motivation]{ref} The <Rayleigh-Ramsay argon density experiment> shows how a small reproducible density discrepancy can reveal a new <chemical element>.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1903
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

One half went to <Henri Becquerel> for discovering spontaneous <radioactivity>. <Pierre Curie> and <Marie Curie> each received one quarter for investigating that radiation.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1903 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official summary: laureates, prize shares and motivation]{ref} <Becquerel's uranium-radiation experiment> and <Curie ionization-current measurement> expose two complementary experimental routes into <radioactivity>.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1902
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Hendrik Antoon Lorentz> and <Pieter Zeeman> shared the award equally for research on magnetic influences on <electromagnetic radiation>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1902 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official summary: laureates, prize shares and motivation]{ref} The <Zeeman discovery experiment of 1896> connects this award to changes in <spectral lines>.

= Nobel Prize in Physics in 1901
{c}
{parent=Nobel Prize in Physics}

<Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen> received the award for discovering <X-rays>.<The Nobel Prize in Physics 1901 by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Official summary: laureates, prize shares and motivation]{ref} The <Röntgen X-ray discovery experiment> follows how a glowing screen led to tests of a new penetrating <electromagnetic radiation>.
